/** @mainpage Qore oracle Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section oracleintro oracle Module Introduction

    The oracle module provides an Oracle driver to Qore's DBI system, allowing Qore
    programs to take access Oracle databases through the Qore's \c Datasource,
    \c DatasourcePool, and \c SQLStatement classes.

    This module is released under the <a href="http://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html">LGPL 2.1</a>
    and is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded
    unconditionally regardless of how the Qore library was initialized).

    Also included with the binary oracle module:
    - @ref oracleextensionsintro "OracleExtensions" user module providing transparent SQL
      tracing support using Oracle's \c DBMS_APPLICATION_INFO package.

    Example of creating an Oracle Datasource (note that \a db_encoding, \a host,
    and \a port are optional - using the hostname and port allows connections
    to be established without TNS, without these parameters TNS is used):

    @code{.py}
    Datasource ds(SQL::DSOracle, user, pass, db, db_encoding, host, port);
    @endcode

    or

    @code{.py}
    Datasource ds("oracle:user/password@db%host:1522");
    @endcode

    The driver supports transactions, bound parameters, and prepared statements.
    See @ref oraclecapabilitiesguide for supported features and their requirements.

    @section oracle_connection_values Connection Values in Error Messages

    The driver's exceptions do not show the connection's user name, password or database name.  The driver's own
    descriptions do not name them, and they are masked as \c **** in the messages of the Oracle client and server,
    which can name them: for example, an unknown service or TNS alias (\c ORA-12514, \c ORA-12154), or the user as
    the schema of an object (\c ORA-00942: <tt>table or view "****"."T" does not exist</tt>).  The \c ORA- code and the
    rest of the message are kept, and a host or port on its own is not masked.

    How the values are matched:
    - the password is matched exactly (Oracle passwords are case-sensitive) wherever it occurs in a message;
    - the user name and the database values are matched without regard to the case of ASCII letters (the server
      shows unquoted names in upper case), and only as whole names: a value is not masked where it is a part of a
      longer name (letters, digits, \c _, \c $ and \c #), so that, for example, the user \c scott does not mask
      \c SCOTTY;
    - the database values are the database string as given and the service, SID and instance names in it (of an
      Easy Connect string or a connect descriptor), or in the definition of the TNS alias that it names (from the
      \c tnsnames.ora files that the Oracle client reads); the parts of a proxy user name (<tt>user[client]</tt>) and
      the name in a quoted user name are matched as well;
    - message codes (for example \c ORA-00942) are kept whole, unless a connection value occurs in one across its
      \c - or past its end (for example a service named \c ERP-20240, which is masked).

    Limits:
    - a value that is a common word also masks that word where it stands alone in a message (for example, a user
      named \c table masks \c TABLE, a database named \c oracle masks \c Oracle in a message, and a password \c a
      masks every \c a);
    - letters other than ASCII letters are matched exactly, and a value is masked only where it occurs as given;
    - the service of a TNS alias is masked only if the alias can be resolved from \c tnsnames.ora as described in
      @ref oracleora_sandbox_connectionsguide (for example, not with an LDAP naming method or
      \c NAMES.DEFAULT_DOMAIN);
    - the Oracle client's own trace and log files (for example \c sqlnet.log, which the client can write to the
      current directory of the process for a failed connection, or its ADR directory) are written by the client and
      are not masked; see the Oracle Net documentation for \c LOG_DIRECTORY_CLIENT, \c DIAG_ADR_ENABLED and
      related \c sqlnet.ora parameters.

